<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cacique">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cacique</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as cazique, cacica, kasike</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A cacique (feminine cacica) was a chief of certain Indigenous peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a>; the word comes from the Kalinago kassiquan, 'to keep house'. Among the Taíno the rank was hereditary. The Spanish used the word for the leader of almost any Indigenous group: early Caribbean caciques such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatuey">Hatuey</a> and Enriquillo eventually revolted, while those of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> became loyal intermediaries of colonial rule, styled don and doña, and some held entailed estates. Mexican independence in 1821 abolished their last privileges; the word later also came to mean a local political boss.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chumayel chronicle speaks of the multepal, the 'joint government' in the city, and a colonial report describes Mayapan as a kind of court where all the <b>caciques</b> and lords of the land resided and brought their tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Muisca government rested on chiefs, whom the Spanish called <b>caciques</b>, and on the kin-based communities beneath them, which the sources call uta and sybyn.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Taíno lived in villages, called yucayeques, under a chief known as a <b>cacique</b>, or cacica when the ruler was a woman, and grouped their chiefdoms, the cacicazgos, into confederations.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The word <b>cacique</b>, which Spanish conquerors applied to native rulers throughout the Americas, was borrowed from the Taíno, whose chiefdoms Columbus met in the Caribbean in 1492.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The crown recognised the old ruling families of central Mexico and the Andes as nobles so long as they were loyal and Catholic: <b>caciques</b> in New Spain, kurakas in Peru, where Inca nobles who proved their descent could be recognised as hidalgos of Castile.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li><li><blockquote>It developed within Taíno society over the centuries before Spanish arrival and by 1500 was the most powerful of the island's chiefdoms, with other <b>caciques</b> recognising its leader.</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-guaynia">Cacicazgo of Guaynía</a></li></ul></section>
